fpcmoh: read the image until the sensor stops sending

This is the fix. Everything the a900 was blamed for -- "goes silent on EP0
after a capture", "resets itself", "cannot re-arm" -- is one driver bug: the
image read loop walks away while the sensor is still transmitting.

fpc_ssm_img_read_cb() consumes exactly one record per SSM state, and both state
machines provide exactly eleven read states (FP_*_WAIT4IMG_SEQ1..SEQ11). The
driver therefore always reads eleven records and then stops, whatever the sensor
still has queued. On the 10a5:a900 there are three more per capture -- measured
from usbmon, immediately after the eleventh record ends on its short packet:

    993 bytes   evt cmdid 5
     81 bytes   evt cmdid 5
     12 bytes   evt cmdid 6

Two separate things go wrong when they are left unread, and both had to be fixed
to get a working login.

1. The sensor's control handler does not service endpoint 0 while an IN transfer
   is outstanding, so the next control command times out whatever it is. 0x02
   (arm), 0x03 (abort) and 0x0A (dead pixel) were each observed hanging, 109 us
   after the last image packet; the same 0x02 completes in 12.7 ms when no
   capture has happened. ~1.08 s later the firmware watchdog resets the sensor,
   which drops off the bus and re-enumerates at a new address -- measured at
   +1.073 s and +1.076 s from the last image packet, and it fires whether or not
   the host sends anything (a run that waited 3 s in silence found the device
   already gone: ENODEV, not a timeout). None of this is command-specific,
   wValue-specific, or caused by the cleanup path, and the sensor is not asleep:
   the bulk endpoint answers throughout, ~100 us per read.

2. These are TLS records, so they must be handed to the enclave rather than just
   drained off the wire. Skipping them desynchronises the TLS sequence
   numbering, every subsequent decrypt fails, and fpc_tee_end_enroll() returns -1
   with a 92-byte template. Draining and discarding was tried first and produces
   an enrollment that passes all eleven stages, reports enroll-completed, stores
   a print -- and then verifies against nothing.

So FP_ENROLL_X_POSTDRAIN / FP_VERIFY_READ_REMAINING sit immediately after each
image loop, before any control transfer, and read and process trailing records
exactly as fpc_ssm_img_read_cb() does, looping until a read times out. The cost
in the normal case is one timeout (200 ms) per capture.

Enabled per device from fpc_dev_probe(), because on a sensor that really does
send exactly eleven records this would add that timeout for nothing. Only the
a900 sets it; the 9800 is untouched.

Applies on top of merge request 396 and patches 0001-0005.
